text:
Annotate an object with a type in TypeScript and its precise shape quietly disappears.

Say you're building a color palette and want the compiler to catch a wrong key or a wrong value shape:

const palette: Record<Colors, string | RGB> = {
  red: [255, 0, 0],
  green: "seagreen",
  blue: [0, 0, 255],
};

palette.green.toUpperCase(); // Error: string | RGB has no toUpperCase

The Record<...> annotation does its job at the definition line, but it also widens every value to string | RGB. palette.green isn't string anymore, it's the whole union, so code that used to just work now needs an unnecessary type guard.

satisfies closes that gap. Swap the annotation for a suffix and the checker still catches a wrong key or shape, but each value keeps its specific inferred type:

const palette = {
  red: [255, 0, 0],
  green: "seagreen",
  blue: [0, 0, 255],
} satisfies Record<Colors, string | RGB>;

palette.green.toUpperCase(); // fine, green is string

as T skips validation entirely. A plain : T annotation validates but widens. satisfies does both: it checks the value against the type and lets inference keep the narrower literal.

My rule of thumb: reach for satisfies on config objects, route maps or lookup tables like this one, any time you still need the specific value types after the check, not just at the point of definition.

What's the last type-widening surprise that cost you a debugging session?
